    "faq": {
      "type": "pvd-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "faq-1": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Are pavers cheaper than decking?", "answer": "<p>Sometimes, and it depends almost entirely on the site. The stones themselves are often inexpensive, but excavation, base material, disposal, equipment, and labor usually make up most of a paver patio's finished cost, and all of them scale with your yard. A deck kit has a known price before you start, and no site earthwork. On a flat, accessible yard with a small footprint, pavers can come in lower; on a sloped or awkward site the comparison usually flips.</p>" } },
        "faq-2": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Is a paver patio a good DIY project?", "answer": "<p>It can be, but it is a bigger job than it looks. Most of the work is in the base: excavating, hauling soil away, spreading and compacting gravel in layers, grading for drainage, and screeding sand perfectly level, usually with a rented plate compactor and masonry saw. Mistakes in the base tend to show up later as sinking, pooling, or shifting stones, and fixing them means lifting the surface.</p>" } },
        "faq-3": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Why do pavers sink?", "answer": "<p>Almost always because of what is underneath. A base that was too thin, unevenly compacted, or washed out by water moving through it lets stones drop below the surrounding surface. The fix is to lift the affected pavers, rebuild and re-compact the base, re-screed the sand, and reset the stones.</p>" } },
        "faq-4": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Can weeds grow between pavers?", "answer": "<p>Yes. Wind-blown seed settles into the joint sand, and ordinary sand gives roots an easy home, so weeds often appear within a season or two. Re-sanding with polymeric sand, which hardens when wet, helps, but pulling weeds and re-sanding joints is a recurring job. A deck surface has no joints for weeds to grow in.</p>" } },
        "faq-5": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Is a deck better for a sloped backyard?", "answer": "<p>Usually. Pavers need level ground, so a slope means grading, extra excavation and disposal, and often a retaining wall. A deck frame sits level on posts and footings whatever the ground does underneath, with longer posts on the low side instead of a wall. A sloped site still needs its footings placed correctly and, for taller decks, local requirements checked.</p>" } },
        "faq-6": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Which needs more maintenance?", "answer": "<p>A paver patio typically needs the most ongoing work: weeding and re-sanding joints, pressure washing, re-sealing every few years, and lifting and resetting areas that settle. A wood deck needs cleaning and staining or sealing every one to three years. A composite deck needs little more than a wash and a yearly check of fasteners and footings.</p>" } },
        "faq-7": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Are pavers or decks better for resale value?", "answer": "<p>Both add value, but decks have recovered more of their cost on average. In the Zonda 2025 Cost vs. Value Report, a wood deck addition recovered 94.9% of its cost at resale and a composite deck 88.5%, compared with roughly 46% for the backyard patio project analyzed. The projects are not identical and none of these are guaranteed returns, so treat the figures as a signal, not a promise.</p>" } },
        "faq-8": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "How long do paver patios last?", "answer": "<p>The stones themselves are durable; how long the patio stays flat and usable depends on the base underneath and how well it is maintained. A well-built, well-maintained paver patio can perform for a long time. A thin or poorly compacted base, poor drainage, or freeze-thaw cycles can lead to settling, heaving, and repairs much sooner.</p>" } },
        "faq-9": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "How long does a wood deck last?", "answer": "<p>It depends on the lumber, the climate, and the upkeep. Pressure-treated wood lasts longest when it is cleaned and stained or sealed every one to three years and the fasteners and footings are checked each spring. A-DIY wood deck kits carry a 5-year structural warranty.</p>" } },
        "faq-10": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "What about composite decking?", "answer": "<p>Composite removes most of the finishing work: no staining or sealing, stains lift easily, and routine care is a hose or light wash. It typically costs more up front than pressure-treated wood. A-DIY composite deck kits are backed by a 25-year warranty.</p>" } },
        "faq-11": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Can I build a deck without hiring a contractor?", "answer": "<p>Yes. An A-DIY kit arrives with pre-engineered, pre-cut frame sections, boards, footings, and hardware, and a drill is the only tool you need. You place the footings on a prepared, reasonably level spot, set the frame, and fasten the boards, with step-by-step guides and a real person on the phone or chat if you get stuck.</p>" } },
        "faq-12": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "What makes A-DIY different from traditional DIY decking?", "answer": "<p>Traditional DIY decking means designing the structure, buying and cutting lumber, and building the frame from scratch. A-DIY is a professional-grade deck engineered into a kit: modular frame sections, pre-cut and pre-drilled boards, and footings included, so assembly is fastening, not fabricating. It ships to your driveway at a price that is the same nationwide and known before you start.</p>" } },
        "faq-13": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Does a deck require permits or special footings?", "answer": "<p>Permit rules vary by city and county, and often depend on the deck's height, size, and whether it attaches to the house, so check with your local building department before you build. A-DIY kits include their footings, which need to be set correctly on a prepared, reasonably level spot; for taller decks or sloped sites, check local requirements first.</p>" } },
        "faq-14": { "type": "question", "settings": { "question": "Can a deck be built in the middle of a backyard?", "answer": "<p>Yes. A freestanding deck does not need to attach to the house, so it can go wherever the yard is best: out by the fire pit, under a tree, or where the evening light is. It still needs a reasonably level spot for its footings and ground underneath that drains.</p>" } }
      },
